                                }
                            },
                            {
                                "box": {
                                    "id": "obj-38",
                                    "linecount": 65,
                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                    "patching_rect": [ 30.0, 553.0, 577.0, 1275.0 ],
                                    "text": "================================================================================\nreese_liquid_5limit — Parameter Information\n================================================================================\n\nWHAT IT IS\nA classic Reese/supersaw bass voice built from three band-limited saws at the\ntuba's pitch: one phase-locked unison core and two voices detuned by a just\n(5-limit) comma. The beating you hear is an exact small-integer ratio — 81/80,\n128/125, 25/24 or 16/15 — not an equal-tempered cent offset, so the stack stays\nin tune with the horn however long the note lasts. A bar-synced two-pole tone\nfilter supplies the \"liquid\" movement.\n\nSource: Timoney, DAFx-14 (oscillator waveform animation / detuned bank);\nWeingartner, DAFx-25 paper 68 §supersaw (multi-saw chorus).\n\nAt mix = 1 the tuba itself is gone; the voice follows the horn's envelope\n(there is a fast-attack / 80 ms-release gate on the input).\n\nPARAMETERS (7 + output gain)\n\n1. detune — 0…100 cents, default 20 cents\n   Inter-voice detune. The value snaps to the nearest 5-limit comma:\n   <10.7 = unison, 21.5 = 81/80, 41.1 = 128/125, 70.7 = 25/24, and\n   91.2–100 = 16/15 (111.7 cents, the top of the range).\n   Small = tight, thick unison; 70 cents = full wide Reese shimmer.\n\n2. motion — 0…1, default 0.35\n   Depth of the bar-locked tone sweep. 0 is a static stack; higher opens and\n   closes the filter once per bar for the slow liquid bass movement.\n\n3. tone_hz — 200…12000 Hz, default 7000 Hz\n   Cutoff of the two-pole lowpass on the stack. Low values give the dark,\n   rounded deep-dubstep saw; high values let the buzz through.\n\n4. glide_ms — 0…200 ms, default 9 ms\n   Pitch-glide time for f0 changes. Short keeps the tuba's articulation;\n   long turns it into a portamento synth.\n\n5. stereo — 0…1, default 0.4\n   Fans the two detuned voices to opposite sides, core stays centred. At 0 the\n   output is mono-compatible.\n\n6. mix — 0…1, default 1\n   Dry tuba against the voice. The unison core is phase-locked to the horn\n   (and to in5 when patched), so the fundamental adds in phase rather than\n   cancelling.\n\n7. out_gain_db — -24…+12 dB, default 0 dB\n   Final trim. Does not count toward the 8-parameter limit.\n\nINLETS / OUTLETS\n- in1 / in2: stereo tuba.\n- in3: f0 in Hz. 0 or out-of-range means \"unknown\": the last valid pitch is\n  held (init 55 Hz). There is no zero-crossing fallback.\n- in4: one-bar phasor. Held at 0 the filter free-runs at 0.12 Hz.\n- in5 (optional): phase-locked tuba phasor. When it moves, the unison core is\n  gently pulled into phase with it; unpatched, f0 integration alone runs the\n  bank.\n- out1 / out2: stereo.\n\nNOTES\n- Voice 2 is at ratio r and voice 3 at r^2; powers of 5-limit ratios are still\n  5-limit, so no ET interval can be dialled in.\n- The saws are polyBLEP-corrected, so the stack does not alias into the sub.\n- No safety limiter. Level is left to the player and out_gain_db.\n",
                                    "varname": "paraminfo_comment"
                                }
                            }
                        ],
