                            },
                            {
                                "box": {
                                    "id": "obj-33",
                                    "linecount": 65,
                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                    "patching_rect": [ 30.0, 553.0, 577.0, 1236.0 ],
                                    "text": "================================================================================\nflanger_through_zero_jet — Parameter Information\n================================================================================\n\nWHAT IT IS\nThe old through-zero tape-flange trick: two taps either side of a reference\ndelay, swept so their spacing passes through zero once per cycle. At the\ncrossing the comb notches slam up to Nyquist and straight back down (a sharp\nbounce, not a gentle turn-around), which is the sweeping \"jet\" sound — far more violent than an ordinary flanger, and the reason\nthis one is the go-to for liquid bass glides and long trip-hop drones.\n\nSource: Huovilainen, DAFx-05 (2005_P_155) §1.1 and §4; flanger block after\nEsqueda/Välimäki/Parker, DAFx-15 §3 Fig. 6; cubic Lagrange fractional delay\nafter Nam & Välimäki, DAFx-09 paper 72 §2.1.\n\n100% wet: both taps are delayed, so no dry copy of the tuba reaches the output.\nNo dry/wet control, no limiter.\n\nPARAMETERS (6)\n\n1. sweep_ms — 0.05…12 ms, default 10.10 ms\n   Half-spread of the two taps. Small = tight metallic sheen on the midrange;\n   large = slow, wide whoosh that reaches down into the low bass. This is the\n   \"size of the jet\".\n\n2. sweep_rate_hz — 0.02…2 Hz, default 0.1 Hz\n   Speed of the sweep: one through-zero \"slam\" per cycle. Below 0.1 Hz it becomes a texture you hear as movement\n   rather than as an LFO — good under long dubstep notes.\n\n3. feedback — -0.95…+0.95, default -0.9\n   Loop gain around the delay line. Negative is the classic hollow,\n   out-of-phase flange; positive is thicker and more resonant. Values near\n   ±0.9 whistle — that is the flanger on the edge, use it deliberately.\n\n4. stereo_offset — 0…1, default 1.0\n   Phase offset between the left and right sweep (1 = half a cycle, 0.25 =\n   an eighth). At 0 the two channels are identical and the effect sits in the\n   middle; higher values make the two sides hit their zero crossings at\n   different moments.\n\n5. tone_hz — 800…14000 Hz, default 8000 Hz\n   One-pole damping inside the feedback loop. Lower keeps the resonance dark\n   and dubby; higher lets the metal ring.\n\n6. out_gain_db — -24…+12 dB, default 0 dB\n   Final trim.\n\nINLETS / OUTLETS\n- in1 / in2: stereo audio.\n- in3: connected but unused — this sweep is time-based, not locked to the note.\n- in4: 0..1 one-bar phasor (not Hz). While it moves the sweep runs exactly one\n  cycle per bar, with the left channel's zero crossing on the downbeat, and\n  sweep_rate_hz is ignored; held constant or unconnected the effect free-runs\n  at sweep_rate_hz. A stepped (non-phasor) signal here will jump the sweep.\n- out1 / out2: stereo, wet only.\n\nNOTES\n- The two taps are centred on a 0.15 ms floor, so the relative delay crosses\n  zero (through-zero) while neither tap ever reads the input directly.\n- Loop damping and the ±0.95 feedback clamp keep the IIR comb bounded; near\n  ±0.95 the resonant peaks are up to ~+26 dB, so trim with out_gain_db.\n- The half-spread is capped at 1600 samples (12 ms up to 133 kHz; ~8.3 ms at\n  192 kHz).\n",
                                    "varname": "paraminfo_comment"
                                }
                            }
                        ],
