                                    "text": "param out_gain_db -4.5 @min -24 @max 12",
                                    "varname": "out_gain_db"
                                }
                            },
                            {
                                "box": {
                                    "id": "obj-38",
                                    "linecount": 60,
                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                    "patching_rect": [ 30.0, 553.0, 577.0, 1178.0 ],
                                    "text": "================================================================================\nbarberpole_comb_phaser — Parameter Information\n================================================================================\n\nWHAT IT IS\nAn endless phaser for space bass. A resonant comb tuned to your note has a\nfrequency shifter inside its feedback loop, so the resonances climb (or\nfall) forever without resetting, like a barber's pole, and the rate is\nlocked to the bar. With width up, the left channel climbs while the right\nfalls.\n\nSource: Esqueda, Välimäki & Parker, DAFx-15 paper 67 §3–4.1 eq. 9–14\n(barberpole flanging and SSB phasing, allpass warp); Wardle, DAFx-98 §6;\nKleimola, DAFx-08 paper 36 §2.3 (allpass dispersion).\nParents: barberpole_comb_ssb_flanger, 11_bode_ssb_freq_shifter.\n\nPARAMETERS (6 + output gain)\n\n1. cycles_per_bar — -8…+8, default 2\n   Barberpole speed in cycles per bar (+ = rising, - = falling, 0 = a\n   static comb). Follows the tempo of the in4 phasor.\n\n2. comb_ratio — 0.5…4 f0 periods, default 3.414\n   Comb length. 1 = resonances on the harmonics (in key); 2 = also on the\n   half-harmonics (sub shimmer); 0.5 = every other harmonic.\n\n3. feedback — 0…95 %, default 88.72 %\n   Resonance. High = ringing, metallic, long tails.\n\n4. warp — 0…90 %, default 40 %\n   Allpass dispersion in the loop: squeezes the resonances together at the\n   bottom and spreads them at the top. The comb stays tuned on f0.\n\n5. width — 0…100 %, default 2 %\n   100 = L rises while R falls; 0 = both move the same way.\n\n6. blend — 0…100 %, default 100 %\n   Dry/wet. 50 % = the classic phaser sum (deepest notches); 100 % = only\n   the shifted resonant comb.\n\n7. out_gain_db — -24…+12 dB, default -4.5 dB\n   Final trim. Does not count toward the parameter limit.\n\nINLETS / OUTLETS\n- in1 / in2: stereo tuba.\n- in3: f0 in Hz. 0 = disconnected -> fixed 55 Hz. No zero-crossing fallback.\n- in4: one-bar phasor (rate; its speed is measured). If held, an internal\n  2 s bar (120 BPM) sets the rate.\n- in5: pitch confidence (0 while silent, ~1 while playing). While it is low\n  the comb tuning is held, so the tail keeps ringing on the last note.\n  Never moved since load = unpatched = treated as 1.\n- out1 / out2: stereo.\n\nNOTES\n- The comb length is corrected for the loop's own phase delay at f0, so the\n  resonance lands exactly on the note at any warp.\n- Inside the loop: 20 Hz–7 kHz band-limit and a soft (tanh) feedback, so it\n  stays stable where the frequency shifter is not exactly unity gain. This\n  is not an output limiter: the output can still run up to about +7 dB at\n  95 % feedback.\n",
                                    "varname": "paraminfo_comment"
                                }
                            }
                        ],
